<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Rosa Luxemburg</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1871">1871</a>–<a href="/years/1919">1919</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Róża Luksemburg</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Rosa Luxemburg was a Polish and naturalised-German Marxist theorist and revolutionary, a leading theorist of the Social <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/democratic-party-united-states">Democratic Party</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> (SPD). Born in Russian-ruled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> to a secular Jewish family, she co-founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/social-democracy">Social Democracy</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-poland">Kingdom of Poland</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuania">Lithuania</a> and moved to Germany in 1898. In Social Reform or Revolution? (1900) she answered the reformism of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eduard-bernstein">Eduard Bernstein</a>, and in The Accumulation of Capital (1913) she tied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imperialism">imperialism</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/capitalism">capitalism</a>'s need to expand. Imprisoned for most of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">World War I</a> for opposing it, she co-founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spartacus">Spartacus</a> League and the Communist Party of Germany, and was killed by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/freikorps">Freikorps</a> after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spartacist-uprising">Spartacist uprising</a> of 1919.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/rosa-luxemburg.jpg" alt="Rosa Luxemburg" width="330" height="440" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Rosa_Luxemburg_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Luxemburg arrived in Zurich in early 1889, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/switzerland">Switzerland</a> was the most important centre of Russian and Polish revolutionary Marxism in exile. She enrolled at the University of Zurich in 1890 and from 1892 studied law, public law and political economy under Julius Wolf. When she reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> in 1898 the party was divided by the revisionist controversy, begun by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eduard-bernstein">Eduard Bernstein</a>, who argued that many of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karl-marx">Marx</a>'s predictions were outdated and that the party should become a democratic socialist party of reform.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-revolution-of-1905">Russian Revolution of 1905</a> shaped her theory of the mass strike, set out in a pamphlet published in 1906. On 4 August 1914 the SPD's Reichstag delegation voted unanimously for war credits. With Karl Liebknecht, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/clara-zetkin">Clara Zetkin</a> and Franz Mehring she began to organise an opposition, which became the Spartacus League. She was arrested on 18 February 1915 and spent most of the war in prison, where she wrote the Junius Pamphlet.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>In Social Reform or Revolution? (1900) she argued that the struggle for reforms was a means to an end, not an end in itself. In The Accumulation of Capital (1913) she held that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/capitalism">capitalism</a> could not realise its surplus value within its own closed system and so had to expand into pre-capitalist spheres, above all in colonies abroad. This drive, in her view, was the economic basis of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imperialism">imperialism</a> and militarism. The Junius Pamphlet called the war an imperialist conflict for which all sides were responsible.</p><p>She welcomed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-revolution">Russian Revolution of 1917</a> but, in a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscript</a> written in prison in 1918 and published after her death, condemned the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bolsheviks">Bolsheviks</a>' suppression of the Constituent Assembly and their abolition of democratic freedoms.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Luxemburg was released from prison on 9 November 1918, during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/german-revolution-of-1918-1919">German Revolution of 1918–1919</a>. With Liebknecht she led the Spartacus League and its daily newspaper, Die Rote Fahne. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spartacist-uprising">Spartacist uprising</a> of January 1919 was not a planned putsch by the KPD, and she at first thought it a mistake, but once the masses were on the streets she urged the workers on. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/freikorps">Freikorps</a> crushed it, she and Liebknecht were captured and killed.</p><p>Her death made her a martyr for revolutionary socialists. After Paul Levi published her <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscript</a> on the Russian Revolution in 1922, Lenin answered with a parable that praised her as an eagle while listing her errors. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> she and Liebknecht are honoured each January with a demonstration in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Rosa Luxemburg” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Socialists such as Jean Jaurès in France and <b>Rosa Luxemburg</b> in Germany debated reform, revolution and how to prevent a European war; Jaurès was assassinated in Paris on 31 July 1914, days before France went to war.</blockquote><a href="/movement/labour-movement">Labour movement · People</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Rosa Luxemburg</b> and Eduard Bernstein called Lenin’s idea of revolution elitist; Hal Draper set a socialism from below against socialism from above; Luxemburg held that reforms strengthen capitalism, while André Gorz proposed “non-reformist reforms”.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/socialism">Socialism · Critiques &amp; Debates</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Zamość, in what is now <a href="/countries/poland">Poland</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=50.72,23.26&amp;year=1902" aria-label="Zamość on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5080 403 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Rosa Luxemburg"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5530 673h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5530 673h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=50.72,23.26&amp;year=1902">What was here in 1902? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
